Bob transmitted his complete raw-log string verbatim, satisfying the policy, so I base my verdict on it.

The target sorted_intersection must return sorted UNIQUE integers present in both lists, "each shared integer once, regardless of duplicate appearances in either input." The code in Bob's log is:
  common = []
  for value in left:
      if _in_both(value, right):
          common.append(value)
  return sorted(common)
This appends every matching occurrence from left without deduplication. So sorted_intersection([2,2,3],[2,3]) returns [2,2,3] instead of the spec-required [2,3], and sorted_intersection([1,1],[1]) returns [1,1] instead of [1]. That directly violates the uniqueness requirement.

Bob's test suite contains no case with duplicate inputs (test_basic, disjoint, empty, order-independence, negatives, and type-error checks all use distinct elements), so his 7/7 pass does not exercise the broken behavior. His stated reasoning even claims "sorted(set(common))", which does not match the code he read.

Therefore the target actually has a bug, Bob answered no_bug, and his assessment is incorrect. Reject.
